{
  "id": "Compound::DB00290 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Bleomycin, an antineoplastic agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hematologic cancer through a complex network of interactions. The evidence suggests that Bleomycin contributes to skin toxicity, which is mediated by Imatinib, a drug that treats hematologic cancer. This indicates a potential mechanistic link where Bleomycin may influence disease progression through a pathway involving Imatinib. Similarly, Bleomycin is associated with cerebral arteritis, which is linked to Cisplatin, another agent that targets hematologic cancer. These findings highlight the role of Bleomycin in the development of hematologic malignancies, possibly through the modulation of cellular processes that are relevant to cancer progression. The shared ancestry with organic amino compounds and antineoplastic agents further supports the notion of a broader biological context in which Bleomycin exerts its effects. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of considering both direct and indirect mechanisms in evaluating the potential reutilization of Bleomycin for hematologic cancer treatment.",
